WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:07.810
Good afternoon, everyone.

00:00:07.810 --> 00:00:11.740
I'm very excited to introduce
our next keynote

00:00:11.740 --> 00:00:15.370
speaker, Joe Cheng,
the CTO of RStudio.

00:00:15.370 --> 00:00:18.340
And since Joe is
talking about Shiny,

00:00:18.340 --> 00:00:20.350
I thought I'd introduce
him with a little story,

00:00:20.350 --> 00:00:23.290
a personal story, about Shiny.

00:00:23.290 --> 00:00:26.520
And as many of you know, I
wrote the book Mastering Shiny,

00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:28.480
and you might think,
well, why did Hadley

00:00:28.480 --> 00:00:30.340
write that and not Joe?

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:33.430
So Joe came to me, I don't know,
like four or five years ago

00:00:33.430 --> 00:00:36.260
and said, "Hey, like, I want
to write a book about Shiny.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:37.220
What should I do?"

00:00:37.220 --> 00:00:40.090
And so I gave him my book
writing advice, which is

00:00:40.090 --> 00:00:41.680
writing a book is really easy.

00:00:41.680 --> 00:00:44.620
All you need to do is write
for an hour a day for a year,

00:00:44.620 --> 00:00:47.590
and at the end of the year
you'll have that book.

00:00:47.590 --> 00:00:50.770
Now, as far as I know,
no one has successfully

00:00:50.770 --> 00:00:52.190
followed that advice.

00:00:52.190 --> 00:00:54.680
So it's provably
terrible advice.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:58.780
And after my advice failed
for Joe for some time,

00:00:58.780 --> 00:01:02.770
I kind of took pity on
him and said, "Oh, hey, I

00:01:02.770 --> 00:01:04.870
love writing books,
I'll write it for you."

00:01:04.870 --> 00:01:07.690
And it kind of cut to
the end of the story.

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:11.140
I wrote mastering Shiny,
and to say thank you,

00:01:11.140 --> 00:01:15.940
Joe built me a bike, which
is the most amazing bicycle

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:18.580
that I've ever
ridden in my life.

00:01:18.580 --> 00:01:21.730
And it seemed to me just
like this perfect example

00:01:21.730 --> 00:01:22.940
of a win-win.

00:01:22.940 --> 00:01:26.470
Like, I got all of the
pleasure of writing a book

00:01:26.470 --> 00:01:29.480
and I didn't have to
build a bicycle myself.

00:01:29.480 --> 00:01:32.770
And Joe got all the pleasure
of building me a bike

00:01:32.770 --> 00:01:34.150
and didn't have to write a book.

00:01:34.150 --> 00:01:36.585
So please welcome Joe Cheng.

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:49.450
Thank you so much,

00:01:49.450 --> 00:01:54.895
Hadley. Well, that's
less funny after he

00:01:54.895 --> 00:01:57.520
told that beautiful story about
the bike, I feel kind of bad now.

00:02:01.020 --> 00:02:03.820
July 31, 2012.

00:02:03.820 --> 00:02:08.810
Was the day that we first
showed Shiny to the world.

00:02:08.810 --> 00:02:12.360
We were at JSM, not
even a scheduled talk.

00:02:12.360 --> 00:02:15.320
Bryan Lewis just lent us 10
minutes at the end of his talk

00:02:15.320 --> 00:02:18.410
and we showed Shiny.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:21.590
And if you consider
that date, July 31st,

00:02:21.590 --> 00:02:24.710
to be the birth of
Shiny, then this

00:02:24.710 --> 00:02:29.660
is a particularly special talk
for me to give to you today.

00:02:29.660 --> 00:02:32.455
Because that means that
Shiny is 10 years old.

00:02:39.400 --> 00:02:44.240
10 years, it's hard to believe.

00:02:44.240 --> 00:02:48.770
So to mark this
anniversary, I thought

00:02:48.770 --> 00:02:52.010
I'd do things a little bit
different, this time, than usual.

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:55.070
I usually focus on what's
coming, what's new,

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:56.750
and I wanted to take
this opportunity

00:02:56.750 --> 00:02:58.250
to talk a little
bit about how Shiny

00:02:58.250 --> 00:03:02.180
started and the story of
how Shiny started really

00:03:02.180 --> 00:03:06.950
begins with Danny Kaplan.

00:03:06.950 --> 00:03:08.172
Oh! Yeah,

00:03:08.172 --> 00:03:09.505
some Danny fans in the audience.

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:16.490
Danny and his students
at Macalester College

00:03:16.490 --> 00:03:19.190
were the very first
beta testers of RStudio IDE.

00:03:19.190 --> 00:03:24.890
This is going way better
than I thought it would.

00:03:27.820 --> 00:03:30.270
And Danny's feedback
was so great.

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.925
His fingerprints are all
over the IDE even to this day.

00:03:32.925 --> 00:03:34.300
You can see all
the parts that he

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:36.160
influenced with his feedback.

00:03:36.160 --> 00:03:40.510
And in early 2011,
Danny asked JJ for a way

00:03:40.510 --> 00:03:44.590
to build interactive
apps in R that he

00:03:44.590 --> 00:03:49.360
could use to demonstrate
statistical and calculus

00:03:49.360 --> 00:03:51.510
concepts.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:55.630
And JJ being JJ, in about a week,

00:03:55.630 --> 00:03:58.150
he built this.

00:03:58.150 --> 00:04:02.080
This is the manipulate
feature in RStudio.

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:07.160
Sort of the proto
Shiny, if you will.

00:04:07.160 --> 00:04:11.240
And manipulate is great because it almost

00:04:11.240 --> 00:04:12.600
couldn't be simpler.

00:04:12.600 --> 00:04:14.840
This is how manipulate works.

00:04:14.840 --> 00:04:17.740
You give it some kind of

00:04:17.740 --> 00:04:22.650
plot generating function or
plot generating expression.

00:04:22.650 --> 00:04:26.400
And include one or
more unbound variables

00:04:26.400 --> 00:04:30.000
that you want the user to
be able to control, then

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.300
for each of those.

00:04:32.300 --> 00:04:34.920
You describe how you
want that to appear.

00:04:34.920 --> 00:04:36.540
So there are a couple of, there's slider,

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:40.320
and there is lookup, a
couple of things like that.

00:04:40.320 --> 00:04:44.510
And this worked great
and Danny ran with it

00:04:44.510 --> 00:04:49.640
and created quite a few
really beautifully thought

00:04:49.640 --> 00:04:53.270
out and assembled
tiny applets that

00:04:53.270 --> 00:04:55.280
illustrated these concepts.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:05:00.160
And he bundled them together
as part of this project mosaic.

00:05:00.160 --> 00:05:03.830
Now it wasn't long, as tends
to happen with software;

00:05:03.830 --> 00:05:07.420
it wasn't long before
some questions started.

00:05:07.420 --> 00:05:10.760
Boy, it sure would be nice to
have a customizable layout.

00:05:10.760 --> 00:05:12.620
Can I have those inputs?

00:05:12.620 --> 00:05:14.400
Show up in two
columns, for example.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:17.250
Could I have more input types?

00:05:17.250 --> 00:05:20.940
Maybe multiple outputs at once
instead of just one plot, maybe

00:05:20.940 --> 00:05:23.190
different kinds of
outputs, like tables,

00:05:23.190 --> 00:05:25.290
and maybe instead of
just showing things,

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:28.530
we could also take some actions
when the user clicked a button

00:05:28.530 --> 00:05:30.180
or something like that.

00:05:30.180 --> 00:05:32.260
And finally...

00:05:32.260 --> 00:05:34.840
Why do people have
to be using RStudio

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:36.920
IDE to use these applets?

00:05:36.920 --> 00:05:38.470
It would be great
to be able to take

00:05:38.470 --> 00:05:42.810
these little interactive
things and move them out.

00:05:42.810 --> 00:05:45.840
All of those totally
reasonable, totally reasonable,

00:05:45.840 --> 00:05:47.790
great suggestions.

00:05:47.790 --> 00:05:53.680
And what he was describing was
a full interactive UI framework.

00:05:53.680 --> 00:05:56.590
Now, that was something
that JJ and I clearly

00:05:56.590 --> 00:05:59.380
had the technical
ability to create.

00:05:59.380 --> 00:06:02.500
But I felt really strongly,
this is not something

00:06:02.500 --> 00:06:03.440
that we should do.

00:06:03.440 --> 00:06:05.030
This felt like a trap to me.

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:11.760
Because UI programming sucks.

00:06:11.760 --> 00:06:14.270
This is how I
honestly felt in 2012.

00:06:14.270 --> 00:06:19.260
I had spent a lot of my
career writing complex UIs.

00:06:19.260 --> 00:06:23.220
And the reason it sucks is
because of this idiom called

00:06:23.220 --> 00:06:24.260
event handlers.

00:06:24.260 --> 00:06:29.870
This is the way all UI
worked in those days.

00:06:29.870 --> 00:06:33.180
The way way event
handlers work is so simple,

00:06:33.180 --> 00:06:36.290
I can explain it
to you right now.

00:06:36.290 --> 00:06:41.900
The idea is this when x
happens, do y. In this example

00:06:41.900 --> 00:06:46.830
x being that some
text box has changed.

00:06:46.830 --> 00:06:48.840
And in response we
want to do y, which

00:06:48.840 --> 00:06:54.790
is update the text of this
status label to some new value.

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:57.590
Very, very simple.

00:06:57.590 --> 00:07:02.600
The problem is when you have
multiple of these in your UI,

00:07:02.600 --> 00:07:04.740
they become hard
to reason about.

00:07:04.740 --> 00:07:06.150
Similar to global variables.

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:08.450
If you've ever been
warmed off of programming

00:07:08.450 --> 00:07:10.940
with extensive use
of global variables,

00:07:10.940 --> 00:07:14.100
it's the reasoning
that becomes hard.

00:07:14.100 --> 00:07:17.970
And this is a huge problem
because as your UI gets

00:07:17.970 --> 00:07:20.670
more complex and the
logic gets more complex,

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:23.670
it starts requiring extremely
high levels of skill

00:07:23.670 --> 00:07:25.430
and discipline to do well.

00:07:25.430 --> 00:07:27.100
It can be done, clearly

00:07:27.100 --> 00:07:29.400
we've had complex
UIs for a long time,

00:07:29.400 --> 00:07:32.280
but it really is
deceptively difficult

00:07:32.280 --> 00:07:37.110
and takes the most disciplined
and talented engineers.

00:07:37.110 --> 00:07:39.510
And if you don't
get it quite right,

00:07:39.510 --> 00:07:42.090
if you don't have that level
of skill and discipline,

00:07:42.090 --> 00:07:44.790
you end up with bugs and
not just normal bugs,

00:07:44.790 --> 00:07:47.580
like freezing and errors,
but more pernicious ones,

00:07:47.580 --> 00:07:53.720
like an app confidently giving
you an answer that's incorrect.

00:07:53.720 --> 00:07:56.090
And that's bad enough
in a normal piece

00:07:56.090 --> 00:07:58.880
of consumer software
but to inflict that

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:01.780
onto the R community?

00:08:01.780 --> 00:08:04.210
To put the tools into their
hands to confidently get

00:08:04.210 --> 00:08:06.280
the wrong answer.

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:09.550
It was unconscionable.

00:08:09.550 --> 00:08:12.990
So we didn't do it.

00:08:12.990 --> 00:08:17.200
And the problem was, as much
as I hated event handlers,

00:08:17.200 --> 00:08:20.080
I couldn't come up with not
only a better alternative,

00:08:20.080 --> 00:08:21.622
but any alternative.

00:08:21.622 --> 00:08:23.830
I had been doing this kind
of programming day and day

00:08:23.830 --> 00:08:26.350
out for years,
constantly thinking

00:08:26.350 --> 00:08:28.330
there must be a
better way than this.

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:33.220
And I could not even
conceive of a better way.

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:34.799
Until around May...

00:08:34.799 --> 00:08:38.570
May 2012.

00:08:38.570 --> 00:08:41.490
Sorry, there's a lot
of dates in this talk.

00:08:41.490 --> 00:08:46.410
In may of 2012, I watched a
screencast for a brand new web

00:08:46.410 --> 00:08:50.420
framework called Meteor.

00:08:50.420 --> 00:08:54.420
And this was the craziest
thing I'd ever seen.

00:08:54.420 --> 00:08:56.580
It blew me away.

00:08:56.580 --> 00:08:59.220
There were so many
ideas in Meteor

00:08:59.220 --> 00:09:02.820
that they were demonstrating
in their code that

00:09:02.820 --> 00:09:06.605
felt like alien technology
to me, or like a magic trick.

00:09:09.320 --> 00:09:12.770
One particular magic
trick among many

00:09:12.770 --> 00:09:16.320
just stuck itself in my brain
and I couldn't dislodge it.

00:09:16.320 --> 00:09:18.260
And it was this.

00:09:18.260 --> 00:09:20.210
Like any other web
framework, Meteor

00:09:20.210 --> 00:09:22.800
had a way for you
to have templates.

00:09:22.800 --> 00:09:26.400
You would write some HTML
markup, and in the middle,

00:09:26.400 --> 00:09:29.750
you would throw in some
dynamic value like a name.

00:09:29.750 --> 00:09:32.937
And this is where in a
traditional web framework,

00:09:32.937 --> 00:09:34.520
you would then attach
an event handler

00:09:34.520 --> 00:09:36.830
to make sure that
template was re-rendered

00:09:36.830 --> 00:09:39.300
at the appropriate time.

00:09:39.300 --> 00:09:43.050
But the media guy
said this, verbatim"

00:09:43.050 --> 00:09:45.950
"I changed the data and
the template updates.

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:47.380
There's no event handlers.

00:09:47.380 --> 00:09:48.840
It just works."

00:09:48.840 --> 00:09:49.955
And he said it like that.

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:55.050
And I was like,

00:09:55.050 --> 00:09:56.690
There's no event handlers?

00:09:56.690 --> 00:09:59.560
It just works?

00:09:59.560 --> 00:10:00.310
What?

00:10:00.310 --> 00:10:02.190
Like...

00:10:02.190 --> 00:10:03.030
How?

00:10:03.030 --> 00:10:04.055
How does that just work?

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:10.340
And that was me.

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:13.610
I mean, for like days
and maybe a week or two

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:16.470
after, I couldn't
shake this thought.

00:10:16.470 --> 00:10:18.432
How do they do that?

00:10:18.432 --> 00:10:19.890
And the only thing
I could think of

00:10:19.890 --> 00:10:21.895
is maybe they just
re-execute everything.

00:10:21.895 --> 00:10:23.520
Every time something
changes, they just

00:10:23.520 --> 00:10:25.570
re-execute all of
their templates.

00:10:25.570 --> 00:10:30.060
But that didn't seem to be
what they were talking about.

00:10:30.060 --> 00:10:32.100
Finally, I couldn't
take it anymore.

00:10:32.100 --> 00:10:33.940
I just went and googled.

00:10:33.940 --> 00:10:34.773
How does Meteor work?

00:10:34.773 --> 00:10:36.357
Don't worry, you're
not supposed to be

00:10:36.357 --> 00:10:37.630
able to see the code here.

00:10:37.630 --> 00:10:40.950
But there was Stack Overflow answer
that pointed to this file

00:10:40.950 --> 00:10:43.330
and said, this is how it works.

00:10:43.330 --> 00:10:44.950
And I just include
the screenshot

00:10:44.950 --> 00:10:49.130
so that you can see
how short it is.

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:51.540
71 lines of code.

00:10:51.540 --> 00:10:54.900
With pretty copious comments.

00:10:54.900 --> 00:11:00.120
So I downloaded the
Meteor repo onto my laptop

00:11:00.120 --> 00:11:02.710
and got on a plane ride
home from a conference.

00:11:02.710 --> 00:11:06.270
And I told myself by the
time I get off this plane,

00:11:06.270 --> 00:11:08.580
I'm going to understand
how this works.

00:11:08.580 --> 00:11:10.200
And I did.

00:11:10.200 --> 00:11:13.530
It's actually pretty
straightforward once you know the trick.

00:11:13.530 --> 00:11:16.050
It's simple, it's elegant.

00:11:16.050 --> 00:11:19.660
And it felt to me like, maybe
these guys are onto something.

00:11:19.660 --> 00:11:21.610
This is actually the
better way to do this.

00:11:21.610 --> 00:11:25.510
Not just a trick, but a
genuinely new way to do things.

00:11:25.510 --> 00:11:29.440
And I was very
impressed, loved it.

00:11:29.440 --> 00:11:32.640
It was super satisfying to
find out how it worked.

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:35.040
And then I close my
laptop and I don't know,

00:11:35.040 --> 00:11:37.350
watched a movie or something.

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:39.270
No dots were connected that day.

00:11:39.270 --> 00:11:40.550
Shiny was not born that day.

00:11:44.480 --> 00:11:48.300
In fact, it was
almost a month later.

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:50.790
When it finally
hit me, and the way

00:11:50.790 --> 00:11:53.250
it hit me was a little weird.

00:11:53.250 --> 00:11:53.860
I swear

00:11:53.860 --> 00:11:54.670
this is true.

00:11:54.670 --> 00:11:56.212
This has never
happened to me before since.

00:11:56.212 --> 00:12:00.610
That morning
I woke up, I opened

00:12:00.610 --> 00:12:04.570
my eyes and the very first
conscious thought in my head

00:12:04.570 --> 00:12:08.152
was an entire web framework.

00:12:08.152 --> 00:12:09.110
Yeah that's not a joke.

00:12:09.110 --> 00:12:11.200
That literally is what happened.

00:12:11.200 --> 00:12:15.630
And I will show you literally
what it looked like.

00:12:15.630 --> 00:12:19.580
It started with a
simple HTML interface.

00:12:19.580 --> 00:12:21.080
The most important
thing was you weren't

00:12:21.080 --> 00:12:23.940
going to have to write any
JavaScript as an app developer.

00:12:23.940 --> 00:12:26.450
All you had to do
is write HTML tags

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:29.480
and include IDs and
classes that would

00:12:29.480 --> 00:12:32.870
indicate where inputs
and outputs were

00:12:32.870 --> 00:12:35.720
and what they were called.

00:12:35.720 --> 00:12:40.100
And we would power that
with reactive server logic

00:12:40.100 --> 00:12:44.170
using that trick from Meteor.

00:12:44.170 --> 00:12:46.090
All you'd have to
do is explain how

00:12:46.090 --> 00:12:50.440
an output is to be created based
on these input values and Shiny

00:12:50.440 --> 00:12:52.010
would take care of
knowing when to...

00:12:52.010 --> 00:12:53.135
You guys know how it works.

00:12:55.300 --> 00:12:58.340
And then a WebSocket connection
to tie them both together.

00:12:58.340 --> 00:13:00.070
And this what I'm showing,

00:13:00.070 --> 00:13:02.030
that was the first
thought in my brain

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:03.647
when I woke up that morning.

00:13:03.647 --> 00:13:04.480
It felt like a gift.

00:13:10.190 --> 00:13:14.170
But the weirdest thing
about that morning,

00:13:14.170 --> 00:13:17.350
wasn't necessarily the
completeness of this vision.

00:13:17.350 --> 00:13:18.180
It was the timing.

00:13:21.020 --> 00:13:22.650
This is, this is terrifying.

00:13:22.650 --> 00:13:25.610
I've never told this
story in public,

00:13:25.610 --> 00:13:28.220
but I thought it was
a thing that I really

00:13:28.220 --> 00:13:31.090
wanted to share with you now.

00:13:31.090 --> 00:13:33.292
That day, June 15th,
2012, was supposed

00:13:33.292 --> 00:13:34.500
to be my last day at RStudio.

00:13:38.470 --> 00:13:41.740
That spring I was just feeling

00:13:41.740 --> 00:13:44.556
Like my... Boy, this is terrifying...

00:13:48.770 --> 00:13:52.790
That spring, I was feeling
like my career had plateaued.

00:13:52.790 --> 00:13:56.770
I was a software engineer
in my mid-thirties.

00:13:56.770 --> 00:13:59.440
I loved the work that JJ
and I were doing together,

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:01.950
but it was just the two of us.

00:14:01.950 --> 00:14:04.380
And all around I looked at
my classmates and people

00:14:04.380 --> 00:14:07.410
that I had been peers
with at past companies,

00:14:07.410 --> 00:14:12.150
and I saw them starting
companies and riding rocket

00:14:12.150 --> 00:14:15.750
ships, you know, up
the corporate ranks

00:14:15.750 --> 00:14:17.850
at the biggest tech
companies and just

00:14:17.850 --> 00:14:20.670
doing the most amazing things
that I would read about

00:14:20.670 --> 00:14:22.860
in the news.

00:14:22.860 --> 00:14:26.770
And I was deathly afraid
of being left behind

00:14:26.770 --> 00:14:31.740
and I felt like in order for
me to feel like I was a success,

00:14:31.740 --> 00:14:35.020
I was going to need to
start my own company.

00:14:35.020 --> 00:14:37.630
So I went to JJ.

00:14:37.630 --> 00:14:39.700
I mean, I've been with
JJ for a long time,

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:42.790
and I opened up to him
about how I was feeling,

00:14:42.790 --> 00:14:46.360
and he said to me, "I
totally understand,

00:14:46.360 --> 00:14:49.390
and if that's what you want
to do, I will support you.

00:14:49.390 --> 00:14:53.070
I'll help you however I can."

00:14:53.070 --> 00:14:55.440
But this was in
the run up to UseR,

00:14:55.440 --> 00:14:58.230
we were trying to get R
Markdown off the ground.

00:14:58.230 --> 00:15:00.210
He had a keynote.

00:15:00.210 --> 00:15:03.690
So my commitment to
him was, I'm going

00:15:03.690 --> 00:15:06.330
to fully focus on RStudio.

00:15:06.330 --> 00:15:08.230
Until UseR is over.

00:15:08.230 --> 00:15:10.770
And as soon as UseR is
over, I'm going to go on leave

00:15:10.770 --> 00:15:12.450
and think about my future.

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:17.450
And if all went well, I'd never
come back from that leave.

00:15:17.450 --> 00:15:19.090
The last day of UseR,

00:15:22.950 --> 00:15:24.033
June 15, 2012,

00:15:24.033 --> 00:15:24.700
it was that day.

00:15:24.700 --> 00:15:26.660
It was that day is my point.

00:15:26.660 --> 00:15:32.160
What was supposed to be
my last day at RStudio.

00:15:32.160 --> 00:15:35.770
And in the blink of an eye.

00:15:35.770 --> 00:15:40.600
When this appeared in my brain.

00:15:40.600 --> 00:15:42.130
It was a light
switch was flipped

00:15:42.130 --> 00:15:47.230
and all those feelings I had
all the fear and insecurity.

00:15:47.230 --> 00:15:50.340
Were just erased in an instant.

00:15:50.340 --> 00:15:55.770
And I was completely
consumed by this curiosity

00:15:55.770 --> 00:15:57.730
of would this work?

00:15:57.730 --> 00:16:00.910
Can we build this?

00:16:00.910 --> 00:16:04.180
And just filled with this
creative drive to see this

00:16:04.180 --> 00:16:06.720
happen.

00:16:06.720 --> 00:16:10.760
And I tell the story because
I want you to understand.

00:16:10.760 --> 00:16:13.440
How profound a gift,

00:16:13.440 --> 00:16:15.350
really, this was the first of
many profound gifts

00:16:15.350 --> 00:16:16.610
that Shiny gave to me.

00:16:16.610 --> 00:16:21.757
I was about to make a pretty
big life and career decision.

00:16:21.757 --> 00:16:23.340
That I think would
have been a mistake

00:16:23.340 --> 00:16:26.250
because I was making it out
of fear and out of insecurity.

00:16:26.250 --> 00:16:28.170
And what Shiny gave
me was something

00:16:28.170 --> 00:16:33.650
to run towards, with
curiosity, with creativity,

00:16:33.650 --> 00:16:35.970
and it's just been such
a magical 10 years.

00:16:35.970 --> 00:16:37.345
I wouldn't trade
it for anything.

00:16:53.840 --> 00:16:54.530
Oh, my gosh.

00:16:54.530 --> 00:17:00.560
OK now, I took this sketch,
you know, jotted it down

00:17:00.560 --> 00:17:04.040
and ran down to find JJ,
you know, we were at UseR

00:17:04.040 --> 00:17:08.150
and I showed him I was
like, look at this.

00:17:08.150 --> 00:17:09.450
And he immediately got it.

00:17:09.450 --> 00:17:11.588
I mean, instantly he
was like, "This is it.

00:17:11.588 --> 00:17:12.630
This is what you work on.

00:17:12.630 --> 00:17:14.088
From now on, forget
everything else

00:17:14.088 --> 00:17:16.579
that you're working on this."

00:17:16.579 --> 00:17:19.079
And he just
requested one change.

00:17:19.079 --> 00:17:22.060
It took him some
time to convince me.

00:17:22.060 --> 00:17:25.000
He said, why are we
making people write HTML?

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:27.290
And I was like, I'm saving
them from JavaScript.

00:17:27.290 --> 00:17:28.125
They can learn HTML.

00:17:32.490 --> 00:17:35.410
But he was pretty insistent
and he was very right.

00:17:35.410 --> 00:17:38.610
And what we ended
up was this writing

00:17:38.610 --> 00:17:41.320
R on both the
client and the server.

00:17:41.320 --> 00:17:44.510
Of course, you can use
HTML if you want to, but,

00:17:44.510 --> 00:17:49.870
this is pretty much what
we have today in Shiny.

00:17:49.870 --> 00:17:55.240
So with this idea in hand, we
spent a frantic seven weeks,

00:17:55.240 --> 00:17:59.260
making Shiny into
a proof of concept

00:17:59.260 --> 00:18:01.450
that we could present at JSM.

00:18:01.450 --> 00:18:03.280
And seven weeks later,
this is what we had.

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:07.360
This is the demo that
we displayed at JSM

00:18:07.360 --> 00:18:10.060
and we had settled on a
tagline that was "Easily

00:18:10.060 --> 00:18:11.920
create interactive web
applications in R,

00:18:11.920 --> 00:18:15.835
without needing to know
HTML, JavaScript and CSS."

00:18:15.835 --> 00:18:17.335
So now we knew that
it was possible.

00:18:20.180 --> 00:18:22.420
But now what?

00:18:22.420 --> 00:18:24.560
Now to me,

00:18:24.560 --> 00:18:26.390
my ambitions for
this was, gee, I

00:18:26.390 --> 00:18:29.690
hope someone uses
this and likes it.

00:18:29.690 --> 00:18:33.290
And JJ, being JJ said,
"Why shouldn't all R users

00:18:33.290 --> 00:18:35.420
be making apps and
why shouldn't Shiny

00:18:35.420 --> 00:18:36.805
be the way that they make them?"

00:18:40.380 --> 00:18:41.880
So the three of us got to work.

00:18:41.880 --> 00:18:45.690
Winston Chang joined the
company just a very short time

00:18:45.690 --> 00:18:48.960
after JSM, and we got to
work, working on Shiny.

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:52.080
And in order for
Shiny to be a success,

00:18:52.080 --> 00:18:55.020
for it to have an impact
on the R community,

00:18:55.020 --> 00:18:58.440
there were three big questions
that we had to answer,

00:18:58.440 --> 00:19:01.870
and the answers had to
be yes, across the board.

00:19:01.870 --> 00:19:05.210
The first question, can
we even build this in R?

00:19:05.210 --> 00:19:06.680
We did the proof of concept.

00:19:06.680 --> 00:19:08.680
Can we take it to production?

00:19:08.680 --> 00:19:10.360
Because the
conventional wisdom was

00:19:10.360 --> 00:19:15.370
that R is a language for
stats, not web frameworks.

00:19:15.370 --> 00:19:19.840
Secondly, are R users
ready to write web apps?

00:19:19.840 --> 00:19:22.782
Because writing web apps is very
different than data analysis.

00:19:22.782 --> 00:19:24.490
There are different
muscles that you need

00:19:24.490 --> 00:19:27.360
to use and Shiny in particular,

00:19:27.360 --> 00:19:31.420
was going to ask people to
learn concepts that nobody,

00:19:31.420 --> 00:19:33.550
almost nobody had seen before.

00:19:33.550 --> 00:19:37.660
Were R users ready to do that?

00:19:37.660 --> 00:19:42.760
And the third was would
the R community respond?

00:19:42.760 --> 00:19:43.335
In 2012,

00:19:43.335 --> 00:19:44.710
I mean, if you
weren't there, it

00:19:44.710 --> 00:19:47.910
might be a little bit
hard to picture, but...

00:19:47.910 --> 00:19:49.690
We were nobodies,
we were unknowns.

00:19:49.690 --> 00:19:52.650
And not only that, RStudio, it was almost a little

00:19:52.650 --> 00:19:55.020
suspicious what we were doing.

00:19:55.020 --> 00:19:58.590
Here's this, like,
weirdly, fully formed IDE

00:19:58.590 --> 00:20:00.240
that they don't charge for.

00:20:00.240 --> 00:20:02.310
There's no pro version.

00:20:02.310 --> 00:20:05.550
They're working on it full-time.

00:20:05.550 --> 00:20:08.910
So people were wondering,
what's the catch?

00:20:08.910 --> 00:20:12.840
And especially in a community
where the GPL and open source

00:20:12.840 --> 00:20:16.260
was so much part
of the ethos, we

00:20:16.260 --> 00:20:20.690
wondered, are people
going to accept us?

00:20:20.690 --> 00:20:23.180
I want to tackle these questions
one by one a little bit

00:20:23.180 --> 00:20:24.060
out of order.

00:20:24.060 --> 00:20:27.480
The first one being, are R
users ready to write web apps?

00:20:27.480 --> 00:20:31.750
And what we learned was this.

00:20:31.750 --> 00:20:33.320
R users are not
afraid to learn.

00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:36.280
I mean, think of who
you need to be to even

00:20:36.280 --> 00:20:38.990
be considering learning Shiny.

00:20:38.990 --> 00:20:41.860
OK you learn statistics.

00:20:41.860 --> 00:20:43.130
You know about visualization.

00:20:43.130 --> 00:20:45.190
You learned to code.

00:20:45.190 --> 00:20:46.870
You know git.

00:20:46.870 --> 00:20:49.430
You probably know the
grammar of graphics.

00:20:49.430 --> 00:20:51.710
Yeah I think they can sit
through a few tutorials

00:20:51.710 --> 00:20:53.720
and read some reference
documentation.

00:20:53.720 --> 00:20:58.450
R users were not afraid and
continue to not be afraid.

00:20:58.450 --> 00:21:02.500
And R users were and are
incredibly motivated by what

00:21:02.500 --> 00:21:03.410
Shiny offers them.

00:21:03.410 --> 00:21:05.243
We did not have to sell
very hard for people

00:21:05.243 --> 00:21:09.250
to realize Shiny was not just
one more tool in their toolbox,

00:21:09.250 --> 00:21:12.250
but it was a force
multiplier for everything

00:21:12.250 --> 00:21:13.990
that they can
already do in R,

00:21:13.990 --> 00:21:17.410
because it meant those
existing skills could

00:21:17.410 --> 00:21:20.230
be exposed to a broader audience
than they could otherwise

00:21:20.230 --> 00:21:21.810
accomplish.

00:21:21.810 --> 00:21:28.910
And Shiny produced "mic
drop" moments for R users

00:21:28.910 --> 00:21:30.470
I'm going to go out a
little bit on a limb

00:21:30.470 --> 00:21:33.540
here and tell me if
this sounds familiar.

00:21:33.540 --> 00:21:35.930
I heard multiple
stories of people

00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:38.870
going into a conference
room with their Shiny app

00:21:38.870 --> 00:21:40.670
and leaving jaws on the floor.

00:21:40.670 --> 00:21:41.920
I'm going to go out on a limb.

00:21:41.920 --> 00:21:43.020
Can I see a show of hands?

00:21:43.020 --> 00:21:46.140
Anyone had this experience
written a Shiny app that.

00:21:46.140 --> 00:21:49.110
Yeah, see, I'm not making it up.

00:21:49.110 --> 00:21:50.915
And that's a huge
motivator for people.

00:21:53.570 --> 00:21:56.535
And something about Shiny feels
within reach to R users,

00:21:56.535 --> 00:21:58.160
even though there
are a lot of concepts

00:21:58.160 --> 00:21:59.118
that you need to learn.

00:21:59.118 --> 00:22:03.110
Somehow it feels like
we wrote this for you.

00:22:03.110 --> 00:22:06.613
We wrote this expecting you
to come along and learn it.

00:22:06.613 --> 00:22:08.780
And that felt different
than TypeScript and React

00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:10.010
and these other web
technologies where

00:22:10.010 --> 00:22:11.927
it felt like this was
really for somebody else

00:22:11.927 --> 00:22:14.173
and you would have to become
somebody else to learn

00:22:14.173 --> 00:22:14.965
this set of skills.

00:22:17.970 --> 00:22:23.130
And these things might
almost seem obvious by now,

00:22:23.130 --> 00:22:25.370
but it wasn't back then.

00:22:25.370 --> 00:22:29.240
And in 2012 to 2015 there
was a Cambrian explosion

00:22:29.240 --> 00:22:32.270
of reactive frameworks in
all different languages

00:22:32.270 --> 00:22:34.790
and most of them are forgotten.

00:22:34.790 --> 00:22:38.120
Because most of them
didn't find a user base

00:22:38.120 --> 00:22:40.280
that was motivated by
what they had to offer

00:22:40.280 --> 00:22:42.740
or they couldn't make it
past the learning curve.

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:44.810
So I just want to be clear.

00:22:44.810 --> 00:22:46.220
I don't take this for granted.

00:22:46.220 --> 00:22:47.370
It's amazing.

00:22:47.370 --> 00:22:50.530
It's amazing that things
worked out this way.

00:22:50.530 --> 00:22:52.270
So the question we
had going into this

00:22:52.270 --> 00:22:55.360
is are R users ready
to write web apps?

00:22:55.360 --> 00:22:57.130
And the conclusion
that I've come to

00:22:57.130 --> 00:22:59.570
is that R has the best
audience for Shiny.

00:22:59.570 --> 00:23:00.195
It's perfect.

00:23:03.670 --> 00:23:05.620
The next question.

00:23:05.620 --> 00:23:09.040
Would the R community respond?

00:23:09.040 --> 00:23:11.790
And instead of telling
you, let me just show you.

00:23:15.040 --> 00:23:17.860
This is just a list
off the top of my head

00:23:17.860 --> 00:23:22.360
the other day of some of
the many amazing packages

00:23:22.360 --> 00:23:25.270
that people have released
that are built on top of Shiny

00:23:25.270 --> 00:23:28.270
and enhance the capabilities
of Shiny in some way.

00:23:28.270 --> 00:23:30.370
And I know that a lot
of you in this audience

00:23:30.370 --> 00:23:32.770
are using these
packages, and the authors

00:23:32.770 --> 00:23:36.860
of some of these packages are
here in the audience as well.

00:23:36.860 --> 00:23:40.130
Maintaining an open source
package that people actually

00:23:40.130 --> 00:23:42.000
use is not an easy feat.

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:44.450
And a lot of people put a lot
of time into the packages,

00:23:44.450 --> 00:23:46.460
not only on this list,
but on all the ones

00:23:46.460 --> 00:23:48.350
that I don't have
room to list here.

00:23:48.350 --> 00:23:51.240
And again, I don't
take that lightly.

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:55.143
That is a huge
vote of confidence

00:23:55.143 --> 00:23:57.560
in the work that we're doing
and the foundation that we've

00:23:57.560 --> 00:24:02.430
built. So I am so grateful
to all these people.

00:24:02.430 --> 00:24:07.650
And there's a GitHub
user called nanxstats

00:24:07.650 --> 00:24:10.200
that has a document
called Awesome Shiny

00:24:10.200 --> 00:24:12.110
Extensions on GitHub.

00:24:12.110 --> 00:24:14.810
And this is a list
of just a whole bunch

00:24:14.810 --> 00:24:18.560
of packages written in R
that extend or enhance Shiny

00:24:18.560 --> 00:24:19.150
in some way.

00:24:22.010 --> 00:24:22.600
There's a few.

00:24:25.610 --> 00:24:29.000
Again, it's humbling to see
how much time and energy

00:24:29.000 --> 00:24:33.380
people have invested in
trying to make Shiny better.

00:24:33.380 --> 00:24:39.230
And as Hadley said,
writing books ain't easy.

00:24:39.230 --> 00:24:41.570
I'm sorry, this is
unscripted, but I

00:24:41.570 --> 00:24:45.860
believe the first advice you
gave me about book writing

00:24:45.860 --> 00:24:48.230
was I asked, how does it
work with getting a publisher

00:24:48.230 --> 00:24:49.760
or whatever?

00:24:49.760 --> 00:24:54.920
And he said, step
one, write a book.

00:24:54.920 --> 00:24:57.230
Step two, get it published.

00:24:57.230 --> 00:25:00.060
There's no step three.

00:25:00.060 --> 00:25:02.100
Yeah, not great advice.

00:25:02.100 --> 00:25:08.420
So I am astounded that there
are I mean, not just three,

00:25:08.420 --> 00:25:11.060
but there there are
several more complete books

00:25:11.060 --> 00:25:14.000
that people have written
about Shiny and again,

00:25:14.000 --> 00:25:16.680
knowing how hard it is
to put together a book,

00:25:16.680 --> 00:25:22.107
I'm in awe that there are people
who are doing this for Shiny.

00:25:22.107 --> 00:25:24.690
And I don't think we can really
talk about the Shiny community

00:25:24.690 --> 00:25:26.850
without mentioning Eric Nantz.

00:25:26.850 --> 00:25:28.393
Is Eric Nantz around
here somewhere?

00:25:28.393 --> 00:25:29.060
Oh, there he is.

00:25:34.770 --> 00:25:39.720
Yeah Eric Nantz might be the
biggest cheerleader for Shiny

00:25:39.720 --> 00:25:43.080
inside or outside of RStudio.

00:25:43.080 --> 00:25:45.180
And among the many
contributions,

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:47.700
contributions that
Eric has made is

00:25:47.700 --> 00:25:50.760
he currently has this Shiny
developer series going.

00:25:50.760 --> 00:25:53.850
It is, what, 30 plus
episodes and counting

00:25:53.850 --> 00:25:56.430
of really interesting
and deep conversations

00:25:56.430 --> 00:25:59.100
with very interesting
and deep people

00:25:59.100 --> 00:26:00.950
around the Shiny community.

00:26:00.950 --> 00:26:04.820
So I definitely encourage
you to check that out.

00:26:04.820 --> 00:26:08.360
And there are so many other
ways people contribute

00:26:08.360 --> 00:26:12.020
that are not necessarily
in those categories.

00:26:12.020 --> 00:26:16.370
Every GitHub issue or PR,
it takes time and energy

00:26:16.370 --> 00:26:19.830
to put together reprexes,
to put together patches.

00:26:19.830 --> 00:26:22.790
And I appreciate
every single one.

00:26:22.790 --> 00:26:24.950
Every year, we hold
a Shiny contest

00:26:24.950 --> 00:26:28.910
and I'm blown away by the
amount of care people put

00:26:28.910 --> 00:26:30.570
into crafting these submissions.

00:26:30.570 --> 00:26:34.290
It clearly means
a lot to people.

00:26:34.290 --> 00:26:37.200
Blog posts, conference
talks, workshops.

00:26:37.200 --> 00:26:41.400
There's so much content that
people put together about Shiny

00:26:41.400 --> 00:26:44.870
and it is not easy.

00:26:44.870 --> 00:26:48.050
There are entire tutorials
that were created

00:26:48.050 --> 00:26:49.360
by people in the community.

00:26:49.360 --> 00:26:52.400
Zev Ross here in the front
row created a tutorial

00:26:52.400 --> 00:26:55.160
talking about how to
use Shiny from scratch

00:26:55.160 --> 00:26:56.790
with a bent toward ggplot.

00:26:56.790 --> 00:26:59.700
And that got a lot of people
into Shiny in the beginning.

00:27:02.240 --> 00:27:05.380
Appsilon, are the
Appsilon guys here?

00:27:05.380 --> 00:27:06.290
The Appsilon folks.

00:27:06.290 --> 00:27:09.290
Yep they did a Shiny conference.

00:27:09.290 --> 00:27:10.290
Didn't see that coming.

00:27:15.260 --> 00:27:18.000
So, I'm sorry.

00:27:18.000 --> 00:27:19.390
You know, it's been 10 years.

00:27:19.390 --> 00:27:22.920
Please, can you
just give me this?

00:27:22.920 --> 00:27:24.803
I'm so grateful to
all these people

00:27:24.803 --> 00:27:27.220
and all the different kinds
of contributions they've made.

00:27:27.220 --> 00:27:29.387
And because I have the
attention of this whole room,

00:27:29.387 --> 00:27:32.050
and a lot of the people in
the community are in this room.

00:27:32.050 --> 00:27:33.780
Can we just take a
second and all say

00:27:33.780 --> 00:27:35.150
thank you to all these people?

00:27:56.030 --> 00:28:00.270
So, if the question was, will
the R community respond?

00:28:00.270 --> 00:28:03.090
I can't even, I can't
even put into words how

00:28:03.090 --> 00:28:06.710
the community has responded.

00:28:06.710 --> 00:28:10.740
And finally, can we
even build it in R?

00:28:10.740 --> 00:28:16.810
In 2012, you might have
heard things like, R is a DSL.

00:28:16.810 --> 00:28:19.570
A domain specific language.

00:28:19.570 --> 00:28:22.995
The alternative to R isn't
Python or C#, it's SAS.

00:28:26.210 --> 00:28:30.490
You never use R for,
you know, a web server.

00:28:30.490 --> 00:28:33.370
These are real quotes
from a real talk

00:28:33.370 --> 00:28:35.890
that was given by John D. Cook,

00:28:35.890 --> 00:28:39.020
at GOTO 2012. And this is
not to pick on John D. Cook.

00:28:39.020 --> 00:28:43.560
These were totally reasonable
statements at the time.

00:28:43.560 --> 00:28:45.990
And what we found
was not just that it

00:28:45.990 --> 00:28:48.570
was going to be possible
to do this in R

00:28:48.570 --> 00:28:51.960
but it was bizarrely
good as a host language,

00:28:51.960 --> 00:28:54.390
for Shiny, bizarrely good.

00:28:54.390 --> 00:28:57.310
And I feel like that's sort
of an extraordinary claim.

00:28:57.310 --> 00:29:01.500
So I will provide some evidence.

00:29:01.500 --> 00:29:04.890
It's going to get a
little bit geeky, so just

00:29:04.890 --> 00:29:08.640
be ready for the shift in tone.

00:29:08.640 --> 00:29:12.490
A lot of languages have
positional and named arguments.

00:29:12.490 --> 00:29:13.590
Programming languages.

00:29:13.590 --> 00:29:16.830
A positional arg like you
see here is like x, right?

00:29:16.830 --> 00:29:17.590
There's no name.

00:29:17.590 --> 00:29:19.590
You just put it with the
right number of commas

00:29:19.590 --> 00:29:21.860
and it gets matched up.

00:29:21.860 --> 00:29:23.270
And then you have
named arguments

00:29:23.270 --> 00:29:25.710
where you specify exactly
what parameter you're passing.

00:29:25.710 --> 00:29:28.550
Height equals twenty in this case.

00:29:28.550 --> 00:29:29.700
And tons of languages,

00:29:29.700 --> 00:29:33.110
tons of languages have both
positional and named arguments.

00:29:33.110 --> 00:29:35.190
But what almost
none of them have.

00:29:35.190 --> 00:29:38.820
None that are in current
use that I could find.

00:29:38.820 --> 00:29:43.710
R allows the named arguments
to be passed first.

00:29:43.710 --> 00:29:46.110
Super random weird quirk.

00:29:46.110 --> 00:29:47.400
Nobody does this.

00:29:47.400 --> 00:29:51.570
This is not considered
a best practice.

00:29:51.570 --> 00:29:52.710
Python doesn't do it.

00:29:52.710 --> 00:29:53.920
Ruby doesn't do it.

00:29:53.920 --> 00:29:55.000
C# doesn't do it.

00:29:55.000 --> 00:29:57.060
None of these languages
that have both named

00:29:57.060 --> 00:30:00.305
and positional args let
you put them in this order.

00:30:00.305 --> 00:30:02.430
And by the way, in R you can
put it in whatever order

00:30:02.430 --> 00:30:03.560
you can mix them.

00:30:03.560 --> 00:30:05.230
It's R, you know.

00:30:08.360 --> 00:30:10.330
And this matters for Shiny.

00:30:10.330 --> 00:30:12.290
It matters for
Shiny because a lot

00:30:12.290 --> 00:30:14.690
of what the UI
layer of Shiny does

00:30:14.690 --> 00:30:19.042
is express HTML and think
about how you express HTML.

00:30:19.042 --> 00:30:20.250
If you're familiar with HTML.

00:30:20.250 --> 00:30:24.010
If not, hopefully you'll
be able to follow along.

00:30:24.010 --> 00:30:26.930
An HTML tag really lets
you express three things.

00:30:26.930 --> 00:30:28.138
The tag itself.

00:30:28.138 --> 00:30:29.680
In this case, on
the outside, we have

00:30:29.680 --> 00:30:33.250
a UL tag for an unordered list.

00:30:33.250 --> 00:30:37.930
Secondly, the attributes for
that tag describing well,

00:30:37.930 --> 00:30:39.202
attributes about the tag.

00:30:39.202 --> 00:30:41.410
In this case, it's saying
that this unordered list is

00:30:41.410 --> 00:30:43.990
for the purpose of navigation.

00:30:43.990 --> 00:30:46.280
Third and last,
you have children.

00:30:46.280 --> 00:30:49.420
What are the things that are
contained inside of this tag?

00:30:49.420 --> 00:30:51.820
But this order is
the right order.

00:30:51.820 --> 00:30:53.540
This is the way we
think about it, right?

00:30:53.540 --> 00:30:54.637
What am I making a UL?

00:30:54.637 --> 00:30:55.970
What are the attributes of that?

00:30:55.970 --> 00:30:56.830
Ul it's an.

00:30:56.830 --> 00:31:00.400
And then what's inside
the UL? Children.

00:31:00.400 --> 00:31:03.010
So if this is the
structure we're aiming at,

00:31:03.010 --> 00:31:07.340
how closely can we
match that in R?

00:31:07.340 --> 00:31:09.380
Pretty close, as it turns out.

00:31:09.380 --> 00:31:11.440
Pretty close.

00:31:11.440 --> 00:31:14.920
It almost looks like we've just
taken away some angle brackets

00:31:14.920 --> 00:31:17.290
and added some parentheses
and some commas.

00:31:17.290 --> 00:31:19.930
Sorry about the commas.

00:31:19.930 --> 00:31:21.050
It's the same order.

00:31:21.050 --> 00:31:22.720
UL first.

00:31:22.720 --> 00:31:24.810
Attribute second.

00:31:24.810 --> 00:31:25.445
Children third.

00:31:28.360 --> 00:31:29.920
And it's only
because we're allowed

00:31:29.920 --> 00:31:32.620
to put the named
arguments that we're

00:31:32.620 --> 00:31:36.110
using for attributes first.

00:31:36.110 --> 00:31:38.740
So just to take a
language at random.

00:31:43.820 --> 00:31:45.270
Let's talk about Python.

00:31:45.270 --> 00:31:48.460
Python has named arguments,
but they have to go last.

00:31:48.460 --> 00:31:52.270
So we end up with tag first.

00:31:52.270 --> 00:31:54.800
Attributes at the bottom.

00:31:54.800 --> 00:31:57.170
And then all the children.

00:31:57.170 --> 00:32:00.620
And that is a problem because
it adds all this space that you

00:32:00.620 --> 00:32:04.230
have to look past in order
to find out what kind of UL,

00:32:04.230 --> 00:32:05.090
you're creating.

00:32:05.090 --> 00:32:06.830
And this example
is just 12 lines,

00:32:06.830 --> 00:32:09.080
but you can imagine if this
is a production dashboard,

00:32:09.080 --> 00:32:13.500
it might be screen fulls
that you have to traverse.

00:32:13.500 --> 00:32:16.010
So I don't want
to put up

00:32:16.010 --> 00:32:17.260
the weakest straw man possible.

00:32:17.260 --> 00:32:18.677
So let's steel man
it a little bit.

00:32:18.677 --> 00:32:21.840
Right so.

00:32:21.840 --> 00:32:25.500
Here is one option that
you could use in Python

00:32:25.500 --> 00:32:26.740
to improve the situation.

00:32:26.740 --> 00:32:28.260
You could get rid
of named arguments

00:32:28.260 --> 00:32:29.635
altogether and
express everything

00:32:29.635 --> 00:32:30.790
with positional arguments.

00:32:30.790 --> 00:32:31.707
And you could do that.

00:32:31.707 --> 00:32:35.580
You could put a dictionary
at the top similar to a named

00:32:35.580 --> 00:32:37.480
list, I guess.

00:32:37.480 --> 00:32:39.530
And this would work.

00:32:39.530 --> 00:32:41.360
It adds some curly braces.

00:32:41.360 --> 00:32:43.570
It adds some quotation marks.

00:32:43.570 --> 00:32:46.385
So it's not perfect,
but it certainly works.

00:32:46.385 --> 00:32:47.760
I'm not to argue
it doesn't work.

00:32:47.760 --> 00:32:52.880
I would call this maybe 85% as
good as what you can do in R.

00:32:52.880 --> 00:32:54.350
The other way you
go in Python is

00:32:54.350 --> 00:32:57.680
to go all named arguments
so you don't pass children

00:32:57.680 --> 00:32:58.560
as unnamed arguments.

00:32:58.560 --> 00:33:02.420
Instead you make a named
argument called children

00:33:02.420 --> 00:33:04.410
and give it a list.

00:33:04.410 --> 00:33:07.110
Again, the details
here aren't important.

00:33:07.110 --> 00:33:10.680
The point is that while
the point is it's worse,

00:33:10.680 --> 00:33:12.660
I guess, that's the point.

00:33:12.660 --> 00:33:16.290
And this one to me is
worse than the previous one

00:33:16.290 --> 00:33:18.510
because it actually pushes
the code off the page.

00:33:18.510 --> 00:33:22.740
We've added a line, a vertical
line and a level of indentation

00:33:22.740 --> 00:33:26.270
for each level of
children at this point.

00:33:26.270 --> 00:33:31.690
OK and also switching
from UI to reactivity.

00:33:31.690 --> 00:33:34.590
Non-standard evaluation
ended up being weirdly,

00:33:34.590 --> 00:33:37.200
bizarrely good for
reactive programming,

00:33:37.200 --> 00:33:41.030
not something that
we had anticipated.

00:33:41.030 --> 00:33:43.970
This is what my sort of
if I could wave a magic

00:33:43.970 --> 00:33:46.287
wand and have a language
to express reactivity in,

00:33:46.287 --> 00:33:47.870
it might look
something like this,

00:33:47.870 --> 00:33:50.310
I want a language keyword
for the word reactive.

00:33:50.310 --> 00:33:52.010
That's an important concept.

00:33:52.010 --> 00:33:55.660
I want to attach a code
block to that keyword.

00:33:55.660 --> 00:33:58.150
And then reading something
out of a reactive expression

00:33:58.150 --> 00:34:01.990
like read_data, say I
want a star operator.

00:34:01.990 --> 00:34:05.270
Sort of like referencing
a pointer and see.

00:34:05.270 --> 00:34:09.199
So this is kind of what,
you know, if I got my wish,

00:34:09.199 --> 00:34:12.290
this is what a perfect language
for reactivity would look like.

00:34:12.290 --> 00:34:15.294
And let's see what we have
in R. It's pretty close.

00:34:17.889 --> 00:34:20.650
In Python, we have to
go a different way.

00:34:20.650 --> 00:34:22.929
Python does not have
non-standard evaluation,

00:34:22.929 --> 00:34:27.670
so you can't pass a bare
block of code to a function

00:34:27.670 --> 00:34:32.080
and expect it to be anything
but a value by the time

00:34:32.080 --> 00:34:34.320
the function gets at it.

00:34:34.320 --> 00:34:36.780
Instead, in Python, you have
to write a function, a named

00:34:36.780 --> 00:34:41.370
function, and then you have
to apply reactivity to it.

00:34:41.370 --> 00:34:43.780
Now, again, I don't want to
leave you with a straw man.

00:34:43.780 --> 00:34:45.449
So here's the steel man.

00:34:45.449 --> 00:34:47.760
Python has a few tricks
of its own up its sleeve,

00:34:47.760 --> 00:34:50.230
and this is called a decorator.

00:34:50.230 --> 00:34:50.980
And you know what?

00:34:50.980 --> 00:34:52.050
This is not bad.

00:34:52.050 --> 00:34:56.730
I'll say this is maybe,
like, 90% as good as R.

00:34:56.730 --> 00:34:58.000
I still don't love that

00:34:58.000 --> 00:34:58.667
it's a function.

00:34:58.667 --> 00:35:00.042
It feels like you
made a function

00:35:00.042 --> 00:35:01.420
and then you made it reactive.

00:35:01.420 --> 00:35:03.120
And I really prefer
how it looks in R

00:35:03.120 --> 00:35:06.710
where what sort of feels like
you've constructed a reactive.

00:35:06.710 --> 00:35:09.830
But I'm not going to argue that
this doesn't work and doesn't

00:35:09.830 --> 00:35:13.300
have appeal to people who
come from this tradition.

00:35:13.300 --> 00:35:18.700
OK so can we even
build it in R?

00:35:18.700 --> 00:35:21.420
R is the best
language for Shiny.

00:35:21.420 --> 00:35:22.580
I will die on this hill.

00:35:22.580 --> 00:35:23.970
R is the best
language for Shiny.

00:35:29.390 --> 00:35:33.800
So those three big
questions were answered.

00:35:33.800 --> 00:35:36.780
Can we even build
it in R?

00:35:36.780 --> 00:35:39.050
R is the best language for
Shiny.

00:35:39.050 --> 00:35:43.370
Are R users ready to write web apps? R has
the best audience for Shiny.

00:35:43.370 --> 00:35:47.570
And will the R community respond?

00:35:47.570 --> 00:35:48.600
To almost an absurd degree, you have responded.

00:35:51.420 --> 00:35:52.830
So, yes.

00:35:52.830 --> 00:35:54.305
Shiny has been a success.

00:35:57.820 --> 00:36:00.430
So I hope that
I've convinced you,

00:36:00.430 --> 00:36:03.550
or at least I've convinced
you that I am convinced that R

00:36:03.550 --> 00:36:07.583
is the best language for Shiny.

00:36:07.583 --> 00:36:09.250
But you know what
they say about Python?

00:36:12.450 --> 00:36:14.495
Python is the second best
language for anything.

00:36:22.990 --> 00:36:24.990
Python is the second best
language for anything,

00:36:24.990 --> 00:36:27.600
and that is not an insult.
That is a compliment.

00:36:27.600 --> 00:36:31.320
That quote was popularized by
Dan Callahan at Python 2018

00:36:31.320 --> 00:36:32.590
during a keynote.

00:36:32.590 --> 00:36:35.010
He said, Python is the second
best language for anything,

00:36:35.010 --> 00:36:37.710
and that is an
amazing aspiration

00:36:37.710 --> 00:36:40.830
because it means that whatever
domain you're operating in,

00:36:40.830 --> 00:36:45.913
whatever weird new technique in
computing you're interested in,

00:36:45.913 --> 00:36:48.330
there's a pretty good chance
that you can stitch something

00:36:48.330 --> 00:36:51.180
together in Python,
and it might not

00:36:51.180 --> 00:36:54.300
be the very best version
or the very best expression

00:36:54.300 --> 00:36:57.150
of that idea, but it
will be available to you

00:36:57.150 --> 00:36:58.600
without going somewhere else.

00:36:58.600 --> 00:37:01.270
And there is undoubtedly
power in that.

00:37:05.240 --> 00:37:07.495
So introducing Shiny for Python.

00:37:21.580 --> 00:37:23.670
Like 45% of you
are real excited.

00:37:29.860 --> 00:37:33.880
Here is an app running
in Shiny for Python.

00:37:33.880 --> 00:37:38.990
This is sort of our July 31,
2012 moment for Shiny for R.

00:37:38.990 --> 00:37:41.700
That's about where we are
today with Shiny for Python.

00:37:45.980 --> 00:37:48.290
I don't expect you to be
able to read this code,

00:37:48.290 --> 00:37:50.800
but if you could,

00:37:50.800 --> 00:37:52.550
And, you know, Shiny
for R and, you know,

00:37:52.550 --> 00:37:56.215
a little bit of Python, this is
going to look pretty familiar.

00:37:56.215 --> 00:37:58.840
There are a lot of concepts that
carry through between the two,

00:37:58.840 --> 00:38:03.160
even though we are dealing
with two separate languages.

00:38:03.160 --> 00:38:07.090
And the server side is
filled with the same kind

00:38:07.090 --> 00:38:11.780
of reactive concepts that you're
used to from Shiny for R.

00:38:11.780 --> 00:38:16.190
And before we go further, I have
to warn you, this is in alpha,

00:38:16.190 --> 00:38:17.750
and it's especially
alpha because we

00:38:17.750 --> 00:38:20.560
did all this work in secret.

00:38:20.560 --> 00:38:22.610
and we're not used
to doing that.

00:38:22.610 --> 00:38:23.753
None of us really like it.

00:38:23.753 --> 00:38:25.920
We're really happy to not
have to do it after today.

00:38:28.440 --> 00:38:32.550
So we are not ready
to call something

00:38:32.550 --> 00:38:36.760
out of alpha until the community
has had a chance to bang on it.

00:38:36.760 --> 00:38:39.500
And we still reserve the
right to change the API.

00:38:39.500 --> 00:38:42.520
So I want to I'm going
to give you that Warning.

00:38:42.520 --> 00:38:45.580
Our design philosophy
for Shiny for Python

00:38:45.580 --> 00:38:49.150
was that it's very similar
conceptually to Shiny for R

00:38:49.150 --> 00:38:51.670
and that wasn't necessarily
a first principle that we

00:38:51.670 --> 00:38:52.540
started with.

00:38:52.540 --> 00:38:57.880
We were open to changing our
ideas as we move from R to Python

00:38:57.880 --> 00:38:58.790
and vice versa.

00:38:58.790 --> 00:39:01.930
But I think what we found
was a lot of the trade offs

00:39:01.930 --> 00:39:05.050
that we had made in R
were pretty universal

00:39:05.050 --> 00:39:06.550
and they felt pretty
good in Python,

00:39:06.550 --> 00:39:09.190
but it's not a slavishly
literal import from R.

00:39:09.190 --> 00:39:12.370
We don't want Shiny for
Python to feel like it

00:39:12.370 --> 00:39:14.180
was written by R users.

00:39:14.180 --> 00:39:16.080
We want it to feel
idiomatically like Python.

00:39:18.910 --> 00:39:22.900
So to give an example of what
I mean when I say, you know,

00:39:22.900 --> 00:39:26.020
Python is the second best
language for anything.

00:39:26.020 --> 00:39:30.800
I tapped our resident
astrophysicist, Dr. Julia Silge

00:39:30.800 --> 00:39:33.610
not a joke.

00:39:33.610 --> 00:39:37.040
And I asked her, you
know, I hear Python maybe

00:39:37.040 --> 00:39:38.000
is big in astronomy,

00:39:38.000 --> 00:39:40.820
is there something we could
do with a Shiny app for that?

00:39:40.820 --> 00:39:44.790
And she had this idea for
an air mass calculator.

00:39:44.790 --> 00:39:47.730
And long story
short, this lets you

00:39:47.730 --> 00:39:50.040
provide a list of
astronomical objects

00:39:50.040 --> 00:39:52.350
you're interested in
observing on a certain day

00:39:52.350 --> 00:39:55.160
and from a certain
location on Earth.

00:39:55.160 --> 00:39:58.760
And this calculator will
tell you what times of night

00:39:58.760 --> 00:40:02.256
are optimal for viewing
that object, including,

00:40:06.440 --> 00:40:09.260
(I'm not an astronomer)

00:40:09.260 --> 00:40:12.260
How much air lies
along your line

00:40:12.260 --> 00:40:14.520
of sight between you and
the object you're viewing?

00:40:14.520 --> 00:40:16.740
Because depending on its
angle from the horizon,

00:40:16.740 --> 00:40:18.990
you're going through a
different amount of atmosphere.

00:40:18.990 --> 00:40:24.190
So that's what this air mass
calculator is showing you.

00:40:24.190 --> 00:40:27.855
Super easy to do
because of AstroPy

00:40:27.855 --> 00:40:30.105
and super easy to turn into
this app because of Shiny.

00:40:32.890 --> 00:40:37.340
So Shiny for Python again
alpha it is available now.

00:40:37.340 --> 00:40:41.050
If you go to shiny.rstudio.com/py, you'll

00:40:41.050 --> 00:40:42.740
find lots of documentation.

00:40:42.740 --> 00:40:45.105
There are examples, there's
articles, there's API docs.

00:40:48.050 --> 00:40:51.420
And I want to talk a little
bit about editor support.

00:40:51.420 --> 00:40:53.280
We expect that most
people are probably

00:40:53.280 --> 00:40:55.620
going to be writing
these things in VS Code

00:40:55.620 --> 00:40:58.350
and we've written an
extension for authoring

00:40:58.350 --> 00:41:00.480
Shiny for Python and VS Code.

00:41:00.480 --> 00:41:03.030
If you use something else
like PyCharm or Jupyter lab,

00:41:03.030 --> 00:41:04.150
that should work too.

00:41:04.150 --> 00:41:09.450
We're a pretty standard Python
web framework in that respect.

00:41:09.450 --> 00:41:11.880
RStudio IDE is going to get
basic support for Shiny for

00:41:11.880 --> 00:41:14.750
Python apps in the next release.

00:41:14.750 --> 00:41:17.790
That'll be mostly in the
form of a run app button.

00:41:17.790 --> 00:41:21.090
And if you are using RStudio
Workbench to have RStudio

00:41:21.090 --> 00:41:24.810
on the server, then the VS
Code and Jupyter lab versions

00:41:24.810 --> 00:41:28.830
that are run on RStudio
Workbench work great

00:41:28.830 --> 00:41:31.920
if you have the current release
of

00:41:31.920 --> 00:41:32.420
RStudio Workbench, Spotted Wakerobin.

00:41:36.060 --> 00:41:38.890
Now because Shiny
for Python is Shiny,

00:41:38.890 --> 00:41:41.540
we have to talk about
deployment options.

00:41:41.540 --> 00:41:43.780
And I'm so pleased.

00:41:43.780 --> 00:41:46.120
I'm so pleased to tell
you, this is working today

00:41:46.120 --> 00:41:50.550
on Shinyapps.io you can deploy
Shiny for Python apps today.

00:41:50.550 --> 00:41:52.920
RStudio Connect already
supports Shiny for Python

00:41:52.920 --> 00:41:55.620
and it's current version,
the one that came out

00:41:55.620 --> 00:41:57.330
earlier this month.

00:41:57.330 --> 00:41:59.450
It's just not documented.

00:41:59.450 --> 00:42:02.070
At our request.

00:42:02.070 --> 00:42:04.770
And rsconnect-python
is the CLI tool you

00:42:04.770 --> 00:42:07.600
use to deploy to those servers.

00:42:07.600 --> 00:42:10.710
And as of today,
assuming everything

00:42:10.710 --> 00:42:14.340
went well, I'm getting the nod.

00:42:14.340 --> 00:42:19.600
Yes, this is available
now in rsconnect-python.

00:42:19.600 --> 00:42:23.698
And of course, as JJ
said this morning,

00:42:23.698 --> 00:42:26.240
we never want to lock up your
work and proprietary solutions.

00:42:26.240 --> 00:42:29.902
So we do always have
an open source option available,

00:42:33.070 --> 00:42:38.480
Oh... It'll be fully released when
I get back to my hotel room.

00:42:38.480 --> 00:42:50.997
There was one more button I needed to hit.
Listen, guys, it's a long talk,

00:42:50.997 --> 00:42:51.955
and I was very nervous.

00:42:56.090 --> 00:42:58.330
Instructions for all
of this is they're

00:42:58.330 --> 00:43:00.080
all available on the
Shiny for Python site

00:43:00.080 --> 00:43:03.100
under the deploy tab.

00:43:03.100 --> 00:43:05.640
There is one more deployment
option for Shiny for Python.

00:43:08.200 --> 00:43:10.320
You can deploy it to
a static web server.

00:43:13.910 --> 00:43:16.940
...wait, what?

00:43:16.940 --> 00:43:19.607
And I'm sorry, I'm going to have
to leave you with that teaser,

00:43:19.607 --> 00:43:21.440
because Winston Chang
is going to talk about

00:43:21.440 --> 00:43:24.068
this tomorrow at 11:30 AM.

00:43:24.068 --> 00:43:25.360
Running Shiny without a server.

00:43:35.530 --> 00:43:38.000
I've got to say,
this is, I mean, this

00:43:38.000 --> 00:43:40.270
is a magic trick of our own.

00:43:40.270 --> 00:43:44.710
When Winston suggested
this technique,

00:43:44.710 --> 00:43:46.510
I actually told
him not to do it.

00:43:46.510 --> 00:43:48.340
I said you are
wasting your time,

00:43:48.340 --> 00:43:50.630
and he proved me
wrong within 48 hours.

00:43:50.630 --> 00:43:53.680
So it should be a
talk to remember.

00:43:53.680 --> 00:43:55.220
I do have to give
a little caveats,

00:43:55.220 --> 00:43:56.220
some restrictions apply,

00:43:56.220 --> 00:43:57.260
not all apps will be eligible,

00:43:57.260 --> 00:43:58.552
serious side effects may occur,

00:43:58.552 --> 00:44:00.195
must be 18 years or older.

00:44:00.195 --> 00:44:01.820
You don't need to be
18 years or older.

00:44:01.820 --> 00:44:03.970
But I'm not joking
about the other ones.

00:44:03.970 --> 00:44:05.440
There are caveats.

00:44:05.440 --> 00:44:07.690
This is not going to be
a universal model for how

00:44:07.690 --> 00:44:11.080
we deploy Shiny for
Python, but the fact

00:44:11.080 --> 00:44:13.610
that this is even
possible is incredible.

00:44:13.610 --> 00:44:16.392
And I think for definitely
some of you in this room,

00:44:16.392 --> 00:44:18.100
it's going to be a
powerful way to deploy

00:44:18.100 --> 00:44:19.300
Shiny for Python apps.

00:44:22.020 --> 00:44:25.500
Now I want to make it clear
that the future of Shiny

00:44:25.500 --> 00:44:27.150
is not Python.

00:44:27.150 --> 00:44:31.200
The future of Shiny
is R and Python.

00:44:31.200 --> 00:44:35.950
And I don't want you to think
that we only have goodies for

00:44:35.950 --> 00:44:39.760
the Python community today.

00:44:39.760 --> 00:44:42.490
Again, a lot of this
work was done in secret,

00:44:42.490 --> 00:44:44.770
so we have to consider it alpha.

00:44:44.770 --> 00:44:48.585
But man, the things
you might have

00:44:48.585 --> 00:44:50.210
known that Shiny for
Python was coming.

00:44:50.210 --> 00:44:52.002
I don't think that this
next one is coming.

00:44:56.030 --> 00:44:58.390
A visual editor for Shiny UI.

00:45:09.505 --> 00:45:11.630
Now, some of you that have
known me for a long time

00:45:11.630 --> 00:45:12.770
might be a little bit surprised.

00:45:12.770 --> 00:45:14.562
I mean, this has been
suggested many times,

00:45:14.562 --> 00:45:21.590
and I have shot it down
sometimes in a principled tone.

00:45:21.590 --> 00:45:24.710
So Nick Strayer is
going to talk about why

00:45:24.710 --> 00:45:28.130
we think that this is the right
time for us to do something

00:45:28.130 --> 00:45:31.250
like this and how we
avoided the pitfalls that I

00:45:31.250 --> 00:45:35.690
was so worried about when
it came to visual editors.

00:45:35.690 --> 00:45:37.340
Greg Swinehart is
a developer who

00:45:37.340 --> 00:45:40.920
has worked with and for RStudio
for gosh like 10 years now.

00:45:40.920 --> 00:45:44.180
And recently Greg
joined the Shiny team

00:45:44.180 --> 00:45:49.700
to help us help you make better
designed, better looking apps.

00:45:49.700 --> 00:45:53.660
So it's early
stages, but Greg is

00:45:53.660 --> 00:45:56.840
going to talk about both some
of his own design principles

00:45:56.840 --> 00:45:59.480
that come to mind as
he looks at Shiny apps

00:45:59.480 --> 00:46:00.840
and how they can be better.

00:46:00.840 --> 00:46:02.690
And also some of the
components that we're

00:46:02.690 --> 00:46:06.290
creating that will
help make it easier

00:46:06.290 --> 00:46:07.860
for you to fall into
a pit of success

00:46:07.860 --> 00:46:09.110
when it comes to your designs.

00:46:12.030 --> 00:46:14.160
And finally, Barrett
Schloerke is going

00:46:14.160 --> 00:46:17.010
to be talking about
Shinytest2.

00:46:17.010 --> 00:46:17.830
This is not secret.

00:46:17.830 --> 00:46:21.270
This is up on cran
and Shinytest2

00:46:21.270 --> 00:46:24.570
is a fundamental part of
the Shiny stack for anybody

00:46:24.570 --> 00:46:31.020
who cares about robustness and
keeping their apps bug-free.

00:46:31.020 --> 00:46:35.370
It's really difficult work,
and it takes a lot of patience

00:46:35.370 --> 00:46:37.360
and it's not
particularly glamorous.

00:46:37.360 --> 00:46:41.190
And I think Barrett Schloerke, I
can't think of a better person

00:46:41.190 --> 00:46:45.050
to tackle these kinds
of hard problems.

00:46:45.050 --> 00:46:45.550
I'm sorry.

00:46:45.550 --> 00:46:46.770
I can't resist this.

00:46:46.770 --> 00:46:53.060
Can anyone dead lift more
than 503 pounds in this room?

00:46:53.060 --> 00:46:55.220
And Barret Schloerke is also
the strongest person

00:46:55.220 --> 00:46:56.085
at rstudio::conf.

00:47:07.770 --> 00:47:09.890
He's also a new dad, but
there's lots of new dads.

00:47:09.890 --> 00:47:11.290
That's 503 pounds.

00:47:11.290 --> 00:47:12.340
That's ridiculous.

00:47:18.830 --> 00:47:19.645
This is team Shiny.

00:47:25.340 --> 00:47:26.340
This is the hardest- I knew

00:47:26.340 --> 00:47:28.010
this going to be the
hardest part of my talk.

00:47:28.010 --> 00:47:30.135
It's my favorite slide and
it was the hardest thing

00:47:30.135 --> 00:47:31.110
to come up with

00:47:31.110 --> 00:47:31.900
anything to say.

00:47:31.900 --> 00:47:33.440
I'm so grateful to this team.

00:47:33.440 --> 00:47:35.648
I'm so grateful for the work
that we've done together

00:47:35.648 --> 00:47:38.070
over the last year.

00:47:38.070 --> 00:47:39.950
All right.

00:47:39.950 --> 00:47:41.630
Thank you very much.

00:47:41.630 --> 00:47:46.520
If you want to learn more
about Shiny for Python,

00:47:46.520 --> 00:47:48.330
you can go to this link
or use the QR code.

00:47:48.330 --> 00:47:52.820
And don't forget these talks
tomorrow by Shiny team members.

00:47:52.820 --> 00:47:55.240
Thank you so much for
10 great years of Shiny.

00:48:28.490 --> 00:48:29.460
Thanks, Joe.

00:48:29.460 --> 00:48:30.830
Thanks, everyone.

00:48:30.830 --> 00:48:33.050
Well, while we wait for
our questions on the slido,

00:48:33.050 --> 00:48:36.050
I had one more comment for
Joe, because during this talk

00:48:36.050 --> 00:48:39.530
I remembered I think it
was in 2012 like Joe,

00:48:39.530 --> 00:48:41.510
it must have been
like at UseR or JSM.

00:48:41.510 --> 00:48:43.190
Like Joe was telling
me about this idea

00:48:43.190 --> 00:48:47.450
he had to do a web app, a web
application framework in R

00:48:47.450 --> 00:48:49.460
and I was like, in my head.

00:48:49.460 --> 00:48:51.800
Yeah Joe that's
JUST what R needs...

00:48:51.800 --> 00:48:54.180
A "web development framework."

00:48:54.180 --> 00:48:58.580
So another case where
I was very, very wrong.

00:48:58.580 --> 00:49:00.980
So first question for you, Joe.

00:49:00.980 --> 00:49:04.910
How did you come up
with the name Shiny?

00:49:04.910 --> 00:49:10.823
Yes so the name Shiny comes
from a show called Firefly.

00:49:18.220 --> 00:49:21.988
A few months before
Shiny became a thing,

00:49:21.988 --> 00:49:24.280
I was laying sick in bed and
I watched the whole series

00:49:24.280 --> 00:49:27.670
Firefly for the first time,
and they used the word shiny

00:49:27.670 --> 00:49:30.520
to mean good.

00:49:30.520 --> 00:49:32.300
They'll say, "yeah,
I'm feeling shiny."

00:49:32.300 --> 00:49:34.498
"Yeah, this job is
going really shiny."

00:49:34.498 --> 00:49:36.040
And I just thought
like, oh, that's... I

00:49:36.040 --> 00:49:38.248
liked how they just sort of
slipped that in all casual.

00:49:38.248 --> 00:49:41.680
And I thought that'd be a cool
name for a project someday.

00:49:41.680 --> 00:49:45.300
The day that I was
creating the GitHub repo.

00:49:45.300 --> 00:49:49.270
For the first commit of what
was to become Shiny on GitHub.

00:49:49.270 --> 00:49:51.450
There's a little name
suggester, right?

00:49:51.450 --> 00:49:53.200
It says "Having trouble
thinking of a name?

00:49:53.200 --> 00:49:54.367
It's blah, blah, blah."

00:49:54.367 --> 00:49:55.420
And it's three segments.

00:49:55.420 --> 00:49:56.878
I don't remember
the first segment,

00:49:56.878 --> 00:50:00.090
but it was like it maybe it was
something like ninja, octo cat,

00:50:00.090 --> 00:50:04.050
Shiny, and I was like, all
right, that's a sign from God.

00:50:04.050 --> 00:50:04.810
It's Shiny.

00:50:04.810 --> 00:50:06.018
That's what it's going to be.

00:50:10.660 --> 00:50:12.870
I know I've asked you
this question myself,

00:50:12.870 --> 00:50:15.335
what do you think of
people calling Shiny, R Shiny?

00:50:20.440 --> 00:50:25.060
Yeah, I think maybe Dean Attali
popularized this and just felt

00:50:25.060 --> 00:50:28.450
like it hit's more
clear, and he really was

00:50:28.450 --> 00:50:30.140
successful in popularizing it.

00:50:30.140 --> 00:50:32.897
And I think for people
in internal to RStudio,

00:50:32.897 --> 00:50:35.480
they'd frequently asked me, hey,
is it R Shiny or is it Shiny?

00:50:35.480 --> 00:50:36.438
And I said, it's Shiny.

00:50:36.438 --> 00:50:38.080
It's not R Shiny,
it's just Shiny.

00:50:38.080 --> 00:50:41.560
And so I think all of us sort
of developed a little bit

00:50:41.560 --> 00:50:42.890
of an aversion to R Shiny.

00:50:42.890 --> 00:50:45.010
But now that there's
Shiny for Python,

00:50:45.010 --> 00:50:46.970
it's going to be hard
not to call it R Shiny.

00:50:46.970 --> 00:50:50.340
So I've made my peace with it.

00:50:50.340 --> 00:50:54.030
So what about... Obviously other
similar frameworks like Dash

00:50:54.030 --> 00:50:55.330
and Streamlit exist...

00:50:55.330 --> 00:50:56.860
Why Shiny for Python?

00:50:56.860 --> 00:50:58.680
Yeah, totally.

00:50:58.680 --> 00:50:59.740
It's a valid question.

00:50:59.740 --> 00:51:00.850
And it's a really
interesting question

00:51:00.850 --> 00:51:02.945
we didn't have to face
with Shiny for R.

00:51:06.020 --> 00:51:10.190
I think that there, as these
frameworks were coming out,

00:51:10.190 --> 00:51:12.290
we looked at them,
each one of them,

00:51:12.290 --> 00:51:16.880
and we were curious, not
even from a competitive point

00:51:16.880 --> 00:51:18.530
of view, but just
out of curiosity,

00:51:18.530 --> 00:51:19.920
what were their approaches?

00:51:19.920 --> 00:51:22.300
What trade offs had they chosen?

00:51:22.300 --> 00:51:24.880
And it turned out that
despite the proliferation

00:51:24.880 --> 00:51:28.930
of these kinds of web frameworks
and Python, all of the trade

00:51:28.930 --> 00:51:30.610
offs that they
chose were actually

00:51:30.610 --> 00:51:33.280
quite different than what
we were doing with Shiny.

00:51:33.280 --> 00:51:37.570
And I think the kinds of
apps that I see people build,

00:51:37.570 --> 00:51:40.900
people in this
room, a lot of them

00:51:40.900 --> 00:51:44.320
would actually be difficult
to build given the trade offs

00:51:44.320 --> 00:51:46.750
inherent in those other.

00:51:46.750 --> 00:51:49.880
You know, this is not
a Python conference.

00:51:49.880 --> 00:51:50.750
It's not Posit Con.

00:51:50.750 --> 00:51:52.670
It's rstudio::conf.

00:51:52.670 --> 00:51:54.520
So I don't want to
belabor it, but I

00:51:54.520 --> 00:51:58.520
do want to say that there
are real differences.

00:52:01.270 --> 00:52:03.850
We think that there's room for
something new in the Python

00:52:03.850 --> 00:52:05.220
world.

00:52:05.220 --> 00:52:05.950
Somewhat related.

00:52:05.950 --> 00:52:09.060
What about py script?

00:52:09.060 --> 00:52:11.850
Oh, the new thing
that Peter Wang presented.

00:52:11.850 --> 00:52:17.610
Yes so py script is a really
interesting piece of technology

00:52:17.610 --> 00:52:18.990
that sort of goes the other way.

00:52:18.990 --> 00:52:22.860
It lets you take an HTML page
that might have Javascript,

00:52:22.860 --> 00:52:27.200
might have whatever in it,
and it lets you embed Python.

00:52:27.200 --> 00:52:32.020
Snippets of code into
your JavaScript apps.

00:52:32.020 --> 00:52:34.430
Basically, I think they
want to replace JavaScript

00:52:34.430 --> 00:52:38.370
as the language of directly
manipulating web apps.

00:52:38.370 --> 00:52:40.260
So that replaces one
part of the stack.

00:52:40.260 --> 00:52:42.050
It replaces the
language JavaScript

00:52:42.050 --> 00:52:44.060
with the language Python.

00:52:44.060 --> 00:52:47.840
So I think that's an
interesting endeavor

00:52:47.840 --> 00:52:52.880
and certainly very impressive
technically and quite different

00:52:52.880 --> 00:52:54.680
than I think what
we're trying to do,

00:52:54.680 --> 00:53:00.830
which is to go from Python
and sort of relieve you

00:53:00.830 --> 00:53:03.410
from having to have to think
about the entire stack.

00:53:03.410 --> 00:53:05.870
So the orchestration of how
data moves back and forth

00:53:05.870 --> 00:53:07.330
between the server
and the client.

00:53:07.330 --> 00:53:11.090
There's just a lot of things
that are part of our stack

00:53:11.090 --> 00:53:13.140
that py script is just

00:53:13.140 --> 00:53:15.210
out of scope.

00:53:15.210 --> 00:53:17.370
Now you mentioned
that we might be

00:53:17.370 --> 00:53:21.150
able to run
Shiny for Python

00:53:21.150 --> 00:53:22.960
without of server.

00:53:22.960 --> 00:53:24.135
What about Shiny for R?

00:53:27.060 --> 00:53:29.400
We sure hope.

00:53:29.400 --> 00:53:31.620
We sure hope so.

00:53:31.620 --> 00:53:38.120
The thing is that right now
the technology that this.

00:53:38.120 --> 00:53:41.870
Is it OK if I say the word?

00:53:41.870 --> 00:53:44.510
This approach that we're taking
for running in the browser,

00:53:44.510 --> 00:53:46.480
it's called Wasm.

00:53:46.480 --> 00:53:50.750
And Python is the second
best language for Wasm.

00:53:50.750 --> 00:53:52.770
Yeah those guys every time.

00:53:52.770 --> 00:53:54.770
Yeah they're the second
best language for Wasm.

00:53:54.770 --> 00:53:58.120
Yeah rust is
probably number one.

00:53:58.120 --> 00:54:03.100
So the question really is
about is R going to have Wasm?

00:54:03.100 --> 00:54:05.940
and the answer is.

00:54:05.940 --> 00:54:08.760
Why don't you answer that?

00:54:08.760 --> 00:54:11.790
Yes so that is something that
we're going to be working on.

00:54:11.790 --> 00:54:14.332
You might want to ask
George Stagg, who's

00:54:14.332 --> 00:54:16.290
recently joined RStudio, is giving a lightning

00:54:16.290 --> 00:54:17.890
talk about this.

00:54:17.890 --> 00:54:20.640
So we are actively
working to bring Wasm for R

00:54:20.640 --> 00:54:23.310
so you can
take any application

00:54:23.310 --> 00:54:26.440
and compile it to
Javascript, basically.

00:54:33.733 --> 00:54:36.400
I feel like we just did a really
bad version of, like, Winston's

00:54:36.400 --> 00:54:39.580
and George's talk.

00:54:39.580 --> 00:54:40.600
Sorry, you should,

00:54:40.600 --> 00:54:41.230
you should,

00:54:41.230 --> 00:54:41.960
you should check them out.

00:54:41.960 --> 00:54:42.840
It's going to be very exciting.

00:54:42.840 --> 00:54:44.673
OK, I've got a high
stress question for you,

00:54:44.673 --> 00:54:48.190
but I'm going to follow it up
with a low stress question.

00:54:48.190 --> 00:54:50.103
So when will the
book that covers

00:54:50.103 --> 00:54:51.520
all of these
exciting new features

00:54:51.520 --> 00:54:55.610
be out Joe? Whenever
Hadley needs a new bike?

00:54:59.610 --> 00:55:02.170
Would it be possible to
use both R and Python

00:55:02.170 --> 00:55:04.630
within a single app?

00:55:04.630 --> 00:55:07.400
Could you use both R and
Python in a single app?

00:55:07.400 --> 00:55:10.800
I mean, I suppose you could
do something with reticulate.

00:55:10.800 --> 00:55:13.500
We know you can because
people do use reticulate

00:55:13.500 --> 00:55:17.260
with Shiny for R and I'm sure
the reverse works as well.

00:55:17.260 --> 00:55:18.940
But yeah,
definitely not something

00:55:18.940 --> 00:55:23.920
that we have thought a lot about
in building Shiny for Python.

00:55:23.920 --> 00:55:26.230
How about other packages
in the kind of Shiny

00:55:26.230 --> 00:55:29.520
ecosystem like Shinytest2 and HTML tools?

00:55:29.520 --> 00:55:31.930
Are you going to bring
those to Python as well?

00:55:31.930 --> 00:55:36.430
Yeah, I sure hope so.

00:55:36.430 --> 00:55:40.180
Again, this is the 2012
moment of Shiny for Python.

00:55:40.180 --> 00:55:41.510
Anything is possible.

00:55:41.510 --> 00:55:43.360
Are those three
big questions going

00:55:43.360 --> 00:55:46.090
to be answered the same way
in the Python community?

00:55:46.090 --> 00:55:47.200
Nobody knows.

00:55:47.200 --> 00:55:49.270
So I think it would
probably be premature

00:55:49.270 --> 00:55:52.870
for me to stand here and say
that all these things are

00:55:52.870 --> 00:55:53.930
going to appear.

00:55:53.930 --> 00:55:55.330
But it is certainly
our intention

00:55:55.330 --> 00:55:58.290
if we're successful in the
fullness of time, for Shiny

00:55:58.290 --> 00:56:00.790
for Python to be a mature and
complete stack in the same way

00:56:00.790 --> 00:56:02.690
that Shiny for R is.

00:56:02.690 --> 00:56:05.000
So sort of following
up on that, is the plan

00:56:05.000 --> 00:56:07.550
to have the features
developed at the same pace

00:56:07.550 --> 00:56:10.340
for both R and Python
in the future?

00:56:10.340 --> 00:56:13.340
Oh will the feature be
developed at the same pace?

00:56:13.340 --> 00:56:17.390
Certainly for features where
Python is catching up to R

00:56:17.390 --> 00:56:20.510
naturally it's going to go much
faster in Python than it ever

00:56:20.510 --> 00:56:23.840
did in R because we're doing
everything for a second time.

00:56:23.840 --> 00:56:28.010
It is just much easier when
you have solved a problem once.

00:56:28.010 --> 00:56:31.490
In terms of new features,
we haven't really

00:56:31.490 --> 00:56:32.870
talked about this,
but I wouldn't

00:56:32.870 --> 00:56:35.900
want to commit to any kind
of artificial, artificially

00:56:35.900 --> 00:56:37.140
enforced parity.

00:56:37.140 --> 00:56:40.040
I mean, I think part of
the point of what we're

00:56:40.040 --> 00:56:42.230
trying to do here
is to not focus

00:56:42.230 --> 00:56:44.820
on keeping these things equal.

00:56:44.820 --> 00:56:47.000
We want them to both
be as good as they

00:56:47.000 --> 00:56:49.230
can be for their
respective communities.

00:56:49.230 --> 00:56:51.188
And we might make a
different set of trade offs

00:56:51.188 --> 00:56:53.147
if we expected that people
were going to switch

00:56:53.147 --> 00:56:54.390
back and forth every day.

00:56:54.390 --> 00:56:57.935
But, no doubt there will be
some people who do that.

00:56:57.935 --> 00:57:00.060
I mean, maybe even people
in this room who do that.

00:57:00.060 --> 00:57:01.730
But I think we really
want to optimize

00:57:01.730 --> 00:57:04.400
for if you're spending a lot
of time in Shiny for R,

00:57:04.400 --> 00:57:05.567
it feels good as an R user.

00:57:05.567 --> 00:57:07.817
And if you spend a lot of
time in Shiny for Python,

00:57:07.817 --> 00:57:09.243
it feels good as a Python user.

00:57:09.243 --> 00:57:11.660
So we are going to try to make
each of those frameworks as

00:57:11.660 --> 00:57:13.370
good as they can be.

00:57:13.370 --> 00:57:16.620
So we'll finish up with a
potentially dangerous question.

00:57:16.620 --> 00:57:19.280
And bear in mind we only
have three minutes left,

00:57:19.280 --> 00:57:22.070
so someone would like
to know, don't go

00:57:22.070 --> 00:57:23.700
into too much technical detail.

00:57:23.700 --> 00:57:27.590
What sort of bike
did you build me?

00:57:27.590 --> 00:57:30.510
I know nothing about the
bike, except it's awesome.

00:57:30.510 --> 00:57:33.500
So this is definitely
a Joe question.

00:57:33.500 --> 00:57:36.170
It is an All-City Space Horse.

00:57:36.170 --> 00:57:42.080
It's a steel, light touring
bike and I blinged it out.

00:57:42.080 --> 00:57:44.437
It was pretty spectacular.

00:57:44.437 --> 00:57:46.020
Did you show a picture
I couldn't see?

00:57:46.020 --> 00:57:47.187
No, I didn't show a picture.

00:57:47.187 --> 00:57:52.430
But I can I'll tweet
a I'll tweet a picture

00:57:52.430 --> 00:57:54.390
so people can appreciate it.

00:57:54.390 --> 00:57:58.400
Yeah, I'll tweet the picture.

00:57:58.400 --> 00:58:00.180
So thank you, Joe.

00:58:00.180 --> 00:58:01.080
Thank you all.

00:58:01.080 --> 00:58:03.620
And enjoy the rest
of the conference.

00:58:03.620 --> 00:58:05.440
Thank you.

